## Why This Is Asked
This question probes end-to-end system design for production NLP under strict latency, privacy, and multilingual requirements, including safeguards against hallucinations.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)
- Hallucination detection and factual verification
- Multilingual adapters and shared encoders
- Privacy constraints and on-device PII prefiltering
- Latency optimization, streaming, drift detection, rollout, and rollback strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-pipeline wiring
async function summarizeChat(chat, userLocale) {
  // 1) On-device PII redaction
  const cleanChat = await redactPII(chat);

  // 2) Retrieve relevant context from KB
  const context = await retrieveContext(cleanChat);

  // 3) Generate summary with RAG
  const summary = await generateSummary(cleanChat, context, userLocale);

  // 4) Hallucination detection via cross-check
  const verification = await verifyAgainstKB(summary, context);

  // 5) Confidence gate
  if (verification.confidence < CONFIDENCE_THRESHOLD) {
    return { status: 'blocked', reason: 'low_confidence' };
  }

  return { status: 'success', summary: verification.summary };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design privacy-conscious NLP systems that run entirely in-browser, handling three languages with minimal computational resources. It emphasizes practical hybrid approaches (rule-based + neural) and on-device labeling loops for continuous improvement without data leaving the user's device.

## Key Concepts
- In-browser NLP with lightweight, privacy-preserving models
- Hybrid architecture: rule-based systems combined with small neural networks
- Multilingual tokenization and cross-language adaptation
- Privacy-preserving data collection through on-device labeling
- Drift monitoring with held-out evaluation data and user feedback loops

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal in-browser classifier skeleton
function predict(text) {
  const t = text.toLowerCase();
  let score = 0;

  // Rule-based sentiment cues
  const positiveWords = ['awesome', 'great', 'excellent', 'good', 'amazing'];
  const negativeWords = ['bad', 'terrible', 'awful', 'poor', 'worst'];

  positiveWords.forEach(w => { if (t.includes(w)) score += 1; });
  negativeWords.forEach(w => { if (t.includes(w)) score -= 1; });

  // Determine sentiment
  if (score > 0) return 'positive';
  if (score < 0) return 'negative';
  return 'neutral';
}
```

